y reason.\n\nLet me also double check the systemd unit discovery description in README: \"Canopy reads each unit's WorkingDirectory and ExecStart and accepts the unit only when the working directory path contains a /misty-step/ segment and the ExecStart binary is executable.\" Code also requires unitName starts with forest@ and ends .service, name non-empty, `systemctl --user show` succeeds, and workDir != \"\" && execPath != \"\". Also `strings.Contains(workDir, \"/misty-step/\")` — yes. And isExecutable(execPath). Good.\n\nNow, the \"with or without an inventory file\" claim: Discovery runs at startup and every 5s regardless of inventory file (App.Start always starts discoveryLoop). At startup, main calls DiscoverLocalInstances and sync before App.Start. Correct.\n\nOne subtle nuance: The \"Zero-config discovery — when no inventory file is loaded, Canopy starts with an empty inventory. Discovery then runs at startup and periodically.\" But the validation `validateInventory` requires at least one instance if LoadInventory is used. For zero-config, inventory is empty. Fine.\n\nAnother subtlety: In `main.go`, after `-listen` override, default listener is set. But README says `-listen <host:port>` overrides the listen address from any source. Yes.\n\nNow let me think about whether the stale check should consider that origin/master advanced *after* the request was created. The request was created at 2026-09-04T19:09:44Z? Wait request time is \"2026-09-04T19:09:44Z\". Master f2c0e7b commit time 14:10:08 -0500 = 19:10:08Z. So master advanced just after the request? Actually request time 19:09:44Z is 24 seconds before master commit 19:10:08Z. So the branch was requested right before master moved. Regardless, now it's stale.\n\nLet me also verify there is no verdict/checks ref already for f1256ae. From ls-remote, checks list does NOT include f1256ae; verdict list does NOT include f1256ae. Request exists. Good. But wait — should I double-check if a verdict/checks ref for f1