 some non-fast-forward, revert that fix. But since we only fast-forward, no risk. The staleness is enough.\\n\\nLet me now prepare the Checks payload and Verdict payload.\\n\\nChecks:\\n- name \\\"test\\\", ok true, exit 0\\n- name \\\"vet\\\", ok true, exit 0\\n\\nTimestamp: need RFC3339. Let me get current time. Use date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ.\\n\\nVerdict: \\\"changes\\\", summary with concrete reasons:\\n- Revision is stale: origin/master (f2c0e7b) is not an ancestor of f1256ae; merge-base is ca9e237. Cannot fast-forward origin/master.\\n- Additionally, README Discovery section misstates development checkout discovery: code in discovery.go accepts a forest.yaml declaration and falls back to PATH/factory binary, but README says requires .forest + executable ./forest.\\n\\nWait, I need to be careful with summary length and avoid overclaiming. But it should be concrete. Let me include both.\\n\\nActually, let me reconsider the discovery README claim. The README line says \\\"A checkout is discovered when it contains both a `.forest` directory and an executable `./forest` binary.\\\" Is that perhaps describing the *legacy* condition and they intended to omit forest.yaml? The branch's commits are \\\"document discovery, precedence, and empty state\\\" and \\\"document discovery reconciliation semantics\\\" and \\\"align discovery docs with explicit-inventory preservation.\\\" The final f1256ae \\\"align discovery docs with explicit-inventory preservation\\\" likely only edited the reconciliation paragraphs. Let me check each commit to see what changed.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_uer8nompky3tbo3fw7ef8rsx","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"git show c74f923 --stat 2>&1; echo '---'; git show c74f923 -- README.md 2>&1 | head -120"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b780f8tsda3a6o1i6kdjg7f8","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"git show 0594739 -- README.md 2>&1 | head -120; echo '---F1256AE---'; git show f1256ae -- README.md 2>&1 | head -160"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro-0813","usage":{"input":1570,"output":1532,"cacheRead":29952,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1381,"totalTokens":33054,"cost":{"input":0.0010362000000000001,"output":0.00303336,"cacheRead":0.000658944,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.004728504}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788549334051,"responseId":"gen-1788549334-3mLnCrLSwuJauw5G9lpw","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_uer8nompky3tbo3fw7ef8rsx","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"commit c74f92311c1d30a997209df0ce242a1cc5f955c9\nAuthor: Iron Forest Builder <builder@forest.invalid>\nDate:   Fri Sep 4 13:13:13 2026 -0500\n\n    docs(canopy): document discovery, precedence, and empty state\n\n README.md | 27 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+)\n---\ncommit c74f92311c1d30a997209df0ce242a1cc5f955c9\nAuthor: Iron Forest Builder <builder@forest.invalid>\nDate:   Fri Sep 4 13:13:13 2026 -0500\n\n    docs(canopy): document discovery, precedence, and empty state\n\ndiff --git a/README.md b/README.md\nindex 2c83e04..d6fe1e0 100644\n--- a/README.md\n+++ b/README.md\n@@ -22,6 +22,8 @@ cp canopy.example.json canopy.json\n go run . -config canopy.json\n ```\n \n+`-config` is optional. Run `go run .` without it to start from zero configuration and use local discovery (see [Discovery](#discovery)).\n+\n Open <http://127.0.0.1:8080>. The default listener is loopback-only. If you bind Canopy to another interface, protect it at the network or reverse-proxy boundary because Run logs can contain sensitive repository output.\n \n Build and verify:\n@@ -32,6 +34,31 @@ go vet ./...\n go build -o canopy .\n ```\n \n+## Discovery\n+\n+Without an inventory file, Canopy still starts and discovers local Iron Forest instances from two sources:\n+\n+- **systemd user units** named `forest@<name>.service`. Canopy reads each unit's `WorkingDirectory` and `ExecStart` and accepts the unit only when the working directory path contains a `/misty-step/` segment and the `ExecStart` binary is executable.\n+- **Development checkouts** under `~/Development/misty-step`. A checkout is discovered when it contains both a `.forest` directory and an executable `./forest` binary.\n+\n+Each discovered instance derives a sanitized id and a title-case label from the unit or checkout name, uses the working directory as its root, and uses the resolved `forest` binary as its executable. Instances are sorted by id.\n+\n+## Configuration precedence\n+\n+Canopy resolves configuration in this order:\n+\n+1. `-config <path>` — load exactly that inventory file. A missing or invalid file is a fatal startup error.\n+2. `./canopy.json` — loaded when `-config` is not set and the file exists. A present but invalid default file is skipped.\n+3. Zero-config discovery — when no inventory file is loaded, Canopy starts with an empty inventory and fills it from [Discovery](#discovery).\n+\n+Discovered instances are appended to any configured inventory and deduplicated by instance id, so a configured id wins over a discovered instance with the same id. `-listen <host:port>` overrides the listen address from any source. Defaults are `127.0.0.1:8080` for the listener, 10 seconds for the fleet interval, and 2 seconds for the selected-instance interval.\n+\n+## Empty state\n+\n+Canopy never renders a failed refresh as a healthy empty view. Before an instance produces its first successful snapshot it is `unknown` and the panel shows a waiting-for-first-observation message instead of healthy data. After a successful snapshot, a later failed or timed-out refresh retains that snapshot and marks the instance `stale`.\n+\n+When no instances are configured or discovered, Canopy still serves the page: the fleet rail reports \"No instances configured.\" and the main panel prompts to add an instance. `/healthz` reports process liveness only and remains `ok` when no instance is reachable.\n+\n ## Inventory\n \n ```json\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788549357909},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_b780f8tsda3a6o1i6kdjg7f8","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"commit 05947394ef72a6ea5154dc8b28595defd2686bfb\nAuthor: Iron Forest Fixer <fixer@forest.invalid>\nDate:   Fri Sep 4 13:27:02 2026 -0500\n\n    docs(canopy): document discovery reconciliation semantics\n\ndiff --git a/README.md b/README.md\nindex d6fe1e0..c5dc6e6 100644\n--- a/README.md\n+++ b/README.md\n@@ -36,22 +36,24 @@ go build -o canopy .\n \n ## Discovery\n \n-Without an inventory file, Canopy still starts and discovers local Iron Forest instances from two sources:\n+Canopy runs local discovery at startup and then every 5 seconds, with or without an inventory file. Discovery finds local Iron Forest instances from two sources:\n \n - **systemd user units** named `forest@<name>.service`. Canopy reads each unit's `WorkingDirectory` and `ExecStart` and accepts the unit only when the working directory path contains a `/misty-step/` segment and the `ExecStart` binary is executable.\n - **Development checkouts** under `~/Development/misty-step`. A checkout is discovered when it contains both a `.forest` directory and an executable `./forest` binary.\n \n Each discovered instance derives a sanitized id and a title-case label from the unit or checkout name, uses the working directory as its root, and uses the resolved `forest` binary as its executable. Instances are sorted by id.\n \n+Each successful discovery scan reconciles the working inventory. When discovery returns at least one instance, that result becomes the working set: a discovered id replaces any previous definition with the discovered one, and a previously known id that is absent from the result is removed.\n+\n ## Configuration precedence\n \n Canopy resolves configuration in this order:\n \n 1. `-config <path>` — load exactly that inventory file. A missing or invalid file is a fatal startup error.\n 2. `./canopy.json` — loaded when `-config` is not set and the file exists. A present but invalid default file is skipped.\n-3. Zero-config discovery — when no inventory file is loaded, Canopy starts with an empty inventory and fills it from [Discovery](#discovery).\n+3. Zero-config discovery — when no inventory file is loaded, Canopy starts with an empty inventory. Discovery then runs at startup and periodically (see [Discovery](#discovery)).\n \n-Discovered instances are appended to any configured inventory and deduplicated by instance id, so a configured id wins over a discovered instance with the same id. `-listen <host:port>` overrides the listen address from any source. Defaults are `127.0.0.1:8080` for the listener, 10 seconds for the fleet interval, and 2 seconds for the selected-instance interval.\n+At startup, discovery is merged into the loaded inventory by appending discovered instances whose ids are not already configured, so a configured id is kept during that first merge. Once the app is running, the periodic discovery loop reconciles the working inventory from each scan: when discovery returns at least one instance, a discovered id replaces the previous definition for that id, and any previous id absent from the discovery result is removed. A configured instance that is also discovered is therefore replaced by the discovered definition, and a configured instance that is not locally discovered (for example a remote SSH instance) is removed from the working set at the next reconciliation. `-listen <host:port>` overrides the listen address from any source. Defaults are `127.0.0.1:8080` for the listener, 10 seconds for the fleet interval, and 2 seconds for the selected-instance interval.\n \n ## Empty state\n \n---F1256AE---\ncommit f1256ae04bceb256bfe2bc64f51cde66bf59dbe4\nAuthor: Iron Forest Fixer <fixer@forest.invalid>\nDate:   Fri Sep 4 13:41:51 2026 -0500\n\n    docs(canopy): align discovery docs with explicit-inventory preservation\n\ndiff --git a/README.md b/README.md\nindex c5dc6e6..30fc7b0 100644\n--- a/README.md\n+++ b/README.md\n@@ -43,7 +43,7 @@ Canopy runs local discovery at startup and then every 5 seconds, with or without\n \n Each discovered instance derives a sanitized id and a title-case label from the unit or checkout name, uses the working directory as its root, and uses the resolved `forest` binary as its executable. Instances are sorted by id.\n \n-Each successful discovery scan reconciles the working inventory. When discovery returns at least one instance, that result becomes the working set: a discovered id replaces any previous definition with the discovered one, and a previously known id that is absent from the result is removed.\n+Each successful discovery scan reconciles the working inventory. When discovery returns at least one instance, only entries Canopy previously added by auto-discovery are refreshed or pruned: an auto-discovered id still present is replaced with the newly discovered definition, and an auto-discovered id that stops appearing is removed. Explicitly configured inventory entries are preserved and are not replaced or removed by discovery (see [Configuration precedence](#configuration-precedence)).\n \n ## Configuration precedence\n \n@@ -53,7 +53,7 @@ Canopy resolves configuration in this order:\n 2. `./canopy.json` — loaded when `-config` is not set and the file exists. A present but invalid default file is skipped.\n 3. Zero-config discovery — when no inventory file is loaded, Canopy starts with an empty inventory. Discovery then runs at startup and periodically (see [Discovery](#discovery)).\n \n-At startup, discovery is merged into the loaded inventory by appending discovered instances whose ids are not already configured, so a configured id is kept during that first merge. Once the app is running, the periodic discovery loop reconciles the working inventory from each scan: when discovery returns at least one instance, a discovered id replaces the previous definition for that id, and any previous id absent from the discovery result is removed. A configured instance that is also discovered is therefore replaced by the discovered definition, and a configured instance that is not locally discovered (for example a remote SSH instance) is removed from the working set at the next reconciliation. `-listen <host:port>` overrides the listen address from any source. Defaults are `127.0.0.1:8080` for the listener, 10 seconds for the fleet interval, and 2 seconds for the selected-instance interval.\n+At startup, discovery is merged into the loaded inventory by appending discovered instances whose ids are not already configured, so a configured id is preserved and is not replaced by the discovered definition. During periodic reconciliation, when discovery returns at least one instance, only entries Canopy previously added by auto-discovery are refreshed or pruned: a still-present auto-discovered id is replaced with the newly discovered definition, and an auto-discovered id that stops appearing is removed. Explicitly configured inventory entries are never removed, and a discovered id that matches an explicitly configured id does not replace it. A configured instance that is not locally discovered (for example a remote SSH instance) therefore remains in the working set. `-listen <host:port>` overrides the listen address from any source. Defaults are `127.0.0.1:8080` for the listener, 10 seconds for the fleet interval, and 2 seconds for the selected-instance interval.\n \n ## Empty state\n \n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788549357909},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So the final README is accurate about reconciliation (matching code d63b907). The only inaccuracy remains the \"Development checkouts\" condition (says .forest + ./forest, but code accepts forest.yaml and fallback). This inaccuracy was introduced in c74f923 and retained through f1256ae. But is it actually an inaccuracy? Let me re-examine `scanDevelopmentRoot` more carefully. It checks:\n\n```\nif !isRegularFile(forestYAML) {\n    fi, err := os.Stat(forestDir)\n    if err != nil || !fi.IsDir() || !isExecutable(forestBin) {\n        continue\n    }\n}\nbinary := resolveForestBinary(repoPath, parent)\n```\n\nSo if forest.yaml IS a regular file, it skips the .forest+./forest check entirely and resolves binary (local ./forest first, then PATH, then factory). This means a checkout with only forest.yaml and no .forest/./forest is discovered (if forest binary resolvable from PATH or factory). If no forest.yaml, then requires .forest dir AND ./forest executable. The README states only the no-forest.yaml path and omits forest.yaml path. So yes, inaccurate.\n\nHowever, is `forest.yaml` in `~/Development/misty-step/<name>/forest.yaml` actually a checkout declaration for Canopy? The comment says \"A checkout is recognized either by a forest.yaml declaration or by the legacy combination of a .forest directory and an executable repo-local ./forest binary.\" This behavior was added by ca9e237 \"discover forest.yaml checkouts with factory fallback\" (which is in the base history and thus in the revision). So docs should mention it. This is a valid finding, severity minor (docs-only). But since we're already blocking on staleness, include it as a secondary reason.\n\nLet me also double check the systemd unit discovery description in README: \"Canopy reads each unit's WorkingDirectory and ExecStart and accepts the unit only when the working directory path contains a /misty-step/ segment and the ExecStart binary is executable.\" Code also requires unitName starts with forest@ and ends .service, name non-empty, `systemctl --user show` succeeds, and workDir != \"\" && execPath != \"\". Also `strings.Contains(workDir, \"/misty-step/\")` — yes. And isExecutable(execPath). Good.\n\nNow, the \"with or without an inventory file\" claim: Discovery runs at startup and every 5s regardless of inventory file (App.Start always starts discoveryLoop). At 